            <div xml:id="d21">

                <pb facs="0105" n="17" sameAs="frus:frus1933v04/pg17"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">710.G 1A/199: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Mexico</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Daniels</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Mexico</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1933-09-29T18:00:00-06:00">September 29, 1933—6
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received 10:50 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>197. Minister for Foreign Affairs says he and the President are considering
                    position Mexico will take on the two matters referred to <pb facs="0106" n="18"
                        xml:id="pg18"/> in your message 137, September 28, 8 p.m. He says he is arm
                    in arm with you in hope of having mediation looking to moratorium. He thinks
                    Monroe Doctrine should include the whole world and referred to Senator Pittman’s
                    recent declaration. As to whether these two propositions will be pressed at
                    Montevideo he says the determination of Mexico’s course has not been fully
                    decided upon and he will give me the policy agreed upon before he leaves. He
                    expects to start October 8. Am writing by air mail substance of
                    conversation.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Daniels</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
